          <tr><td>Azul: Summer Pavilion</td><td>2-4</td><td>30-45 min</td><td>Couples and two-player nights</td><td>$35 to $40</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Azul: Stained Glass of Sintra</td><td>2-4</td><td>30-45 min</td><td>Groups that want more bite</td><td>$30 to $38</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Azul Mini</td><td>2-4</td><td>30-45 min</td><td>Travel and small tables</td><td>$18 to $25</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Azul Duel</td><td>2</td><td>about 30 min</td><td>Two players only</td><td>$28 to $35</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Start with <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Azul+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Azul</a>, the 2017 original by Michael Kiesling. It runs about $32, seats 2 to 4, and it is the version every review and every shelf reference means. Between $28 and $35 is a normal price. The full stats live on my <a href="/game/azul">Azul page</a>.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. Two-player Azul is a knife. With only two people the middle of the table is thinner, denial is stronger, and an early floor line is nearly unrecoverable. If you mostly play with one other person, read the next paragraph instead.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Azul+Summer+Pavilion+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Azul: Summer Pavilion</a> is the one I hand to couples. It keeps the same pick and place bones, then adds wild tiles and a more forgiving middle, so a bad round is a scratch instead of a knockout. It usually sits around $35 to $40. New to the shelf and already the game my two-player nights reach for, and it has a page of its own at <a href="/game/azul-summer-pavilion">Azul: Summer Pavilion</a>.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. The wild tiles make the first game feel easier than it is, and the scoring board has more moving parts than the original. Teach it after you have played the base game once, not before.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Azul+Stained+Glass+of+Sintra+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Azul: Stained Glass of Sintra</a> is the mean one, released in 2018. It adds a moving glazier pawn and a mechanic that can crush your own finished glass, so the table talk gets personal. Around $30 to $38. It shows up in my roundup of <a href="/blog/best-strategy-board-games">strategy games</a> for groups who find the base game too polite.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. It is harder to teach and harder to love quickly. Two of the four people I taught it to asked for the original back the same night.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Azul+Mini+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Azul Mini</a> is the same game in a smaller box with smaller tiles and travel trays that hold everything in place. Usually $18 to $25, and it plays identically. I keep it in a bag for cafe tables that are the size of a laptop.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. Small tiles mean small hands only. My group has dropped more than one tile under a table, and the trays are tight enough that you need a moment to set them up.</p>

      <p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Azul+Duel+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Azul Duel</a> is the 2025 two-player game, and it is the sharpest of the family. Every kind of tile has a power, the board is tight, and there is no middle-of-the-table truce. It runs about $28 to $35, and if you want the head-to-head depth of the <a href="/blog/best-2-player-board-games">2-player games</a> list, this belongs on it.</p>

      <p>One honest warning. It teaches slower than the original. The rulebook is thin but the tile powers add up, so plan a full first game just for learning.</p>

      <h2>The Shelf Test</h2>
      <p>Here is the Shelf Test. Would this game survive 10 plays on a shelf that already holds 40 boxes? Azul passes with room to spare, and it passes for a reason most modern games cannot match: setups take 90 seconds, the rules never need to be reopened after night one, and the same four people get sharper instead of bored. Ten plays in, you stop counting tiles you want and start counting tiles you are leaving. That is the moment the game opens up.</p>

      <p>Summer Pavilion earns its spot right next to it, mostly because it plays so well with two. Stained Glass of Sintra is the one box on this list I would sell before the others.</p>

      <p class="cta">Here is the part that matters. Get the base <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Azul+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Azul</a> first, around $32, and put it on the table with the leftover rule already in your head. If your game nights are two people, get <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Azul+Summer+Pavilion+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Azul: Summer Pavilion</a> instead. Catch the base under $28 or Summer Pavilion under $35 and you have done well, since both move with every sale. If you are still picking a first modern game, my <a href="/blog/best-board-games-for-beginners">beginner list</a> starts where this one ends, and the full <a href="/games/strategy">strategy lineup</a> covers what to buy after Azul stops surprising you.</p>

